Subject: Problems resolving Windows Icon position in more recent releases 
of Windows (part 3)
To: pepswin <Unknown>


Up to the end of part 2, we established the problem can be easily solved 
using medium icons and the non-standard view option of disable auto-arrange 
with the recommendation to retain align to grid.  In both Windows XP and 
Windows Vista these are freely available [and will be documented in the 
PEPS manual including color graphics].

Unfortunately, while Windows 7 builds on some frills over XP started in 
Vista, support for the vital feature we need was dropped from the desktop.

That said, and thanks to some very clever Windows 7 users, all is not 
lost.  The underpinnings for waking up these features is still there!  Some 
registry work is needed to wake them up.  I am working on PEPS-style 
bullet-proof implementation of the patches; I presently use the works of 
these two clever authors to fully restore the capability.

There is only one caveat.

Temporary changes to the desktop resolution while the PEPS Command window 
is open will ruin the specific arrangement; so far, I cannot locate a way 
to restore the positions other than manually.

Worse still, permanent desktop resolution changes that require a reboot [or 
at least a logoff/logon cycle[ also ruin it, but with good reason.  You 
will likely have to change something yourself such as the overall window 
size which necessitates changing your preferences, etc.

Other than avoiding these small problems, and worst case spending a small 
amount of time restoring the lost positioning, this is otherwise quite 
stable, although perhaps the exact windows size might need a tweak while 
still maintaining the proper positioning, etc.

Thus, I will add this feature to the SETUP separate window when time 
permits.

Note:  This will NOT use the regedit program.  Instead the command-line 
variant REG.EXE will be part of a batch script.  Unfortunately, the 
commands have to be rewritten because there are quirky incompatibilities 
with regedit, but they are analogous overall, etc.  This involves certain 
parameters in hex in one program but decimal in the other, etc.

Windows 8 I don't currently have access to, and of course most people hate 
it because it lacks a start menu, but to my knowledge, a similar approach 
works there.  This will be confirmed but I am confident it should work.

One of the two authors of the patches has reported that currently, Windows 
10 no longer supports any attempt to do this!  You can make the changes, 
but when the window closes, the changes are all reset!

I will attempt to overcome this in Windows 10 by one of several methods, 
each with trade-offs.

a) The amount of text on a medium icon is very limited.  But Windows 10 
supports long tiles.  You still get forced auto-arrange, but you can set 
the window size.  By embracing the longer tiles, each tile can have a 
convention of a sequence number.  It will be visible and a slight 
distraction, but it will force the order to that which everyone wants, 
relatively speaking.  Additionally, no empty positions are possible, so 
dummy numbered tiles must be included.  This will be somewhat of a PITA 
unless the user opts to make all active project files use the highest 
sequence numbers.  My personal preference places them somewhat in the 
middle, which will be a problem for me, but can be maintained with 
additional effort.  I will provide an example to my liking more like my 
example serving suggestions and also an alternate example with all of the 
active pairs of tiles at the end to minimize the overhead.

b) Install a VM and run XP there, problem solved inside of the virtual 
machine.  To my knowledge you have to purchase a VM program that is 
useful.  Microsoft's only runs in Windows 7 where we don;t need it!

c) devote a large area of the desktop to the icons.  There are means to 
save and restore the desktop and auto-arrange can be defeated.  Just not 
nice when you use your laptop for multiple independent purposes having to 
explain why you have these strange four-dozen icons on your machine's 
desktop!

Of course, these become fixed icons by personal preference, as opposed to a 
Commands window you can move around or collapse to the taskbar, etc.

More to come when I get to try this out on Windows 10, but I am 
disappointed; I was waiting to hear better news from that author, but he is 
now pissed at Microsoft for removing the feature that worked before the 
"Features" update ironically named since it permanently removes certain 
features!
[This is Windows 10 version1903]

cjl




